  "problem_statement": "ReceiveBuffer missing maybe_extract_at_most method breaks HTTP parsing\n\n#### Description\n\nAfter a recent change, the `ReceiveBuffer` class is missing the `maybe_extract_at_most` method, which causes HTTP parsing to fail with AttributeError when trying to read fixed-length content.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport h11\n\n# Create a simple HTTP/1.1 request with content-length\nc = h11.Connection(h11.CLIENT)\nc.send(h11.Request(method='POST', target='/', headers=[('Host', 'example.com'), ('Content-Length', '5')]))\nc.send(h11.Data(data=b'hello'))\nc.send(h11.EndOfMessage())\n\n# Try to parse the response\nserver = h11.Connection(h11.SERVER)\nserver.receive_data(c.data_to_send())\nevents = []\nwhile True:\n    event = server.next_event()\n    if event is h11.NEED_DATA:\n        break\n    events.append(event)\n```\n\nThis will raise an AttributeError about the missing `maybe_extract_at_most` method when the HTTP parser tries to read content with a fixed Content-Length.\n\nThe error also occurs when parsing chunked transfer encoding or any scenario where the HTTP parser needs to extract a specific number of bytes from the receive buffer."
}